ecisely, for a finite I\\subseteq {\\mathbb{Z}}^{+}, let m=max(I\\cup \\left\\{0\\right\\}). Then for n>m, we can count the number of permutations with descent set I that we will denote by d(I,n)=\\left|D(I,n)\\right|=\\left|\\{\\pi \\in {\\mathcal{S}}_{n}\\mid Des\\left(\\pi \\right)=I\\}\\right|.\n\nIn 1915, this function was shown to be a degree m polynomial in n by MacMahon [6]. The descents of permutations gained popularity after Solomon [8] proved that the sums of permutations with the same descent set form a linear basis for a subalgebra of the group algebra called the Solomon algebra (the result is true for any Coxeter group when one extends the definition of descents). Then, in 2013, Billey, Burdzy, and Sagan [1] proved combinatorial results about a similar notion called peaks of permutations. For a permutation \\pi \\in {\\mathcal{S}}_{n} the set of indexes Peak\\left(\\pi \\right)=\\{i\\in [2,n-1]\\mid {\\pi }_{i-1}<{\\pi }_{i}>{\\pi }_{i+1}\\} are the peak positions. They have proved that the counting function P(S,n)=\\left|\\{\\pi \\in {S}_{n}\\mid Peak\\left(\\pi \\right)=S\\}\\right|is a product of a polynomial {p}_{S}\\left(n\\right) and the exponential {2}^{n-\\#S-1}. Also in the same paper, they proposed some conjectures about the coefficients of {p}_{S}\\left(n\\right) in some bases. The zeros and coefficients of the peak polynomial were further investigated by Billy, Fahrbach and Talmage in [2] and by Diaz-Lopez et al. in [4]. In 2019, Diaz-Lopez et al. [5] used similar techniques to find some recursions of descent polynomials. In this paper, we answer several of their conjectures.\n\nThe coefficient sequence {a}_{k}\\left(I\\right) is defined uniquely through the following equation: d(I,n)=\\sum _{k=0}^{m}{a}_{k}\\left(I\\right)\\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0ex}{}{n-m}{k}\\right).In [5], it was shown that the sequence {a}_{k}\\left(I\\right) is nonnegative since it counts some combinatorial objects. ## 2. Preliminaries\n\nIn this section, we will recall some recursions of the descent polynomial associated with a set I and we will establish some related coefficient sequences by choosing different bases for the polynomials.\n\nFirst of all, for the rest of the paper we will always denote a finite subset of {\\mathbb{Z}}^{+} by I, the maximal element of I\\cup \\left\\{0\\right\\} by m\\left(I\\right). If it is clear from the context, m\\left(I\\right) will be simplified to m.\n\nLet us define the coefficients {a}_{k}\\left(I\\right),{c}_{k}\\left(I\\right) for any I with maximal element m and k\\in \\mathbb{N} through the following expressions: d(I,n)=\\sum _{k=0}^{m}{a}_{k}\\left(I\\right)\\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0ex}{}{n-m}{k}\\right)=\\sum _{k=0}^{m}{(-1)}^{m-k}{c}_{k}\\left(I\\right)\\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0ex}{}{n+1}{k}\\right),if k\\le m, and {c}_{k}\\left(I\\right)={a}_{k}\\left(I\\right)=0 if k>m. Observe that they are well-defined, since {\\left\\{\\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0ex}{}{n-m}{k}\\right)\\right\\}}_{k\\in \\mathbb{N}} and also {\\left\\{\\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0ex}{}{n+1}{k}\\right)\\right\\}}_{k\\in \\mathbb{N}} form a basis of the space of one-variable polynomials. Henceforth, we will refer to the first and second bases as “a-basis” and “c-basis”, respectively. We will also consider another basis that is also a Newton-basis.\n\nAs it turns out, these coefficients are integers, moreover, they are nonnegative. To be more precise, in [5] it has been proved that {a}_{k}\\left(I\\right) counts some combinatorial objects (i.e. they are nonnegative integers), and {c}_{0}\\left(I\\right) is nonnegative. The authors of [5] also conjectured that each {c}_{k}\\left(I\\right)\\ge 0, and for a proof of the affirmative answer, see Proposition 4.3.\n\nNext, we would like to establish two recurrences for the descent polynomial, which will be intensively used in several proofs. Before that, we need the following notations. For an 0̸\\ne I=\\{{i}_{1},\\dots ,{i}_{l}\\} and 1\\le t\\le l, let {I}^{-}=I-\\left\\{{i}_{l}\\right\\},{I}_{t}=\\{{i}_{1},\\dots ,{i}_{t-1},{i}_{t}-1,\\dots ,{i}_{l}-1\\}-\\left\\{0\\right\\},{\\widehat{I}}_{t}=\\{{i}_{1},\\dots ,{i}_{t-1},{i}_{t+1}-1,\\dots ,{i}_{l}-1\\},{I}^{\\prime }=\\{{i}_{j}\\mid {i}_{j}-1\\notin I\\},{I}^{\\prime \\prime }={I}^{\\prime }-\\left\\{1\\right\\}.\n\nProposition 2.1\n\n## [5]\n\nIf I\\ne 0̸, then d(I,n)=\\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0ex}{}{n}{m}\\right)d({I}^{-},m)-d({I}^{-},n).\n\nIn contrast to the simplicity of this recursion, the disadvantage is that the descent polynomial of I is a difference of two polynomials. In [5], the authors found another way to write d(I,n) as a sum of polynomials (Thm 2.4 of [5]). Now we will state an equivalent form, which will fit our purposes better, and we also give its proof.\n\nCorollary 2.2\n\nIf I\\ne 0̸, then (2.1)d(I,n+1)=d(I,n)+\\sum _{{i}_{t}\\in {I}^{\\prime \\prime }\\setminus \\left\\{m\\right\\}}d({I}_{t},n)+\\sum _{{i}_{t}\\in {I}^{\\prime }\\setminus \\left\\{m\\right\\}}d({\\stackrel{ˆ}{I}}_{t},n)+d({I}^{-},m-1)\\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0ex}{}{n}{m-1}\\right).\n\n\nCorollary 5.3\n\nIf {z}_{0} is a root of d(I,z), then \\left|{z}_{0}\\right|\\le m.\n\nProof\n\nLet us consider the polynomial p\\left(z\\right)=(z-1)\\stackrel{̄}{a}(I,z), and let {p}_{i} (resp. {\\stackrel{̄}{a}}_{i}) be the coefficient of {z}^{i} in p (resp. \\stackrel{̄}{a}), i.e. p\\left(z\\right)=\\sum _{i=0}^{m}{p}_{i}{z}^{i}\\text{, and }\\stackrel{̄}{a}(I,z)=\\sum _{i=0}^{m-1}{\\stackrel{̄}{a}}_{i}{z}^{i}.The relation of p and \\stackrel{̄}{a} translates as follows: {p}_{i}=\\left\\{\\begin{array}{cc}{\\stackrel{̄}{a}}_{m-1},\\hfill & \\hfill \\text{if }i=m\\text{,}\\hfill \\\\ {\\stackrel{̄}{a}}_{i-1}-{\\stackrel{̄}{a}}_{i},\\hfill & \\hfill \\text{if }0<i<m\\text{,}\\hfill \\\\ -{\\stackrel{̄}{a}}_{0},\\hfill & \\hfill \\text{if }i=0\\text{,}\\hfill \\end{array}\\right.and d(I,n)=\\sum _{k=0}^{m}{p}_{k}\\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0ex}{}{n-m+k}{k}\\right).Since the coefficient sequence of \\stackrel{̄}{a}(I,z) is non-decreasing by Corollary 3.2, all coefficients of p except {p}_{m} are non-positive and their sum is 0. In other words, for any k\\in \\{0,1,\\dots ,m-1\\}: \\left|{p}_{k}\\right|=-{p}_{k},and \\sum _{k=0}^{m-1}\\left|{p}_{k}\\right|=-\\sum _{k=0}^{m-1}{p}_{k}={a}_{m-1}={p}_{m}>0.Therefore, by Lemma 5.2, if \\left|z\\right|>m, then d(I,z)=\\sum _{k=0}^{m}{p}_{k}\\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0ex}{}{z-m+k}{k}\\right)\\ne 0.\\phantom{\\rule{1.2em}{0ex}}\\square \n\nWe would like to remark two facts about the previous proof. First of all, the introduced “new” coefficients, {\\stackrel{̃}{c}}_{k}\\left(I\\right), are exactly {\\stackrel{̃}{c}}_{k}\\left(I\\right)=d({I}^{c},k)=\\left\\{\\begin{array}{cc}\\hfill {(-1)}^{m+|[k+1,\\infty )\\cap I|+k}d(I\\cap [k-1],k),\\hfill & \\hfill \\text{ if }k\\in I\\text{,}\\hfill \\\\ \\hfill 0\\hfill & \\hfill \\text{ otherwise,}\\hfill \\end{array}\\right.where {I}^{c}=\\left[m\\right]\\setminus I, therefore, d(I,n)=\\sum _{k=0}^{m}{(-1)}^{m-k}{\\stackrel{̃}{c}}_{k}\\left(I\\right)\\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0ex}{}{n}{k}\\right)=\\sum _{k=0}^{m}{(-1)}^{m-k}d({I}^{c},k)\\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0ex}{}{n}{k}\\right).\n\nSecond, we cannot extend the proof for any I, because of the crucial statement that was that {\\sum }_{k=0}^{m-1}|{c}_{k}\\left(I\\right)-{c}_{k+1}\\left(I\\right)|\\le {c}_{m}\\left(I\\right) is not true for any I\\subseteq {\\mathbb{Z}}^{+}. (E.g. I=\\{1,2,3,4,5\\})\n\nFrom now on, we would like to understand the roots of I’s with “non-trivial endings”. To analyze these cases, for the rest of the paper we introduce the following notation: for any finite set I\\subseteq {\\mathbb{Z}}^{+} and t\\in \\mathbb{N} let {I}^{t}=I\\cup \\{m+1,m+2,\\dots ,m+t\\}.\n## 6. Some remarks and further directions\n\nWe described an interesting phenomenon in Section 4, namely that {c}_{k}\\left(I\\right) and {(-1)}^{m}d(I,-n) are nonnegative integers. This result suggests that there might be some combinatorial proofs for them.\n\nQuestion 6.1\n\nWhat do the coefficients {c}_{k}\\left(I\\right) and evaluations {(-1)}^{m}d(I,-n) count?\n\nThere are two conjectures about the roots of the descent polynomial that have been presented:\n\nProposition 6.2\n\n## Conjecture 4.3 of [5]\n\nIf {z}_{0} is a root of d(I,n), then\n\n  * •\n\n\\left|{z}_{0}\\right|\\le m,\n\n  * •\n\n\\Re {z}_{0}\\ge -1.\n\nThis conjecture can be viewed as a special case of Theorem 5.10. As a common generalization of the two parts, we conjecture that (motivated by numerical computations for m\\le 13 (e.g. see red regions on Fig. 2), by a proof for the case \\left|I\\right|=1, and by Proposition 5.11) the roots of d(I,m) will be in a disk with the endpoints of one of its diameters being -1 and m. More precisely:\n\nConjecture 6.3\n\nIf d(I,{z}_{0})=0, then |{z}_{0}-\\frac{m-1}{2}|\\le \\frac{m+1}{2}.\n\nSimilarly to the descent polynomial, instead of counting permutations with a described descent set, one could ask for the number of permutations with described positions of peaks (i.e. {\\pi }_{i-1}<{\\pi }_{i}>{\\pi }_{i+1}). As it turns out, this peak-counting function is not a polynomial. However, it can be written as a product of a polynomial and an exponential function in a “natural way”. (See the precise definition in [1].) This polynomial is the so-called peak polynomial. This polynomial behaves quite similarly to the descent one, thus, it is natural to ask whether there is a deeper connection between them, or whether we can prove similar propositions to the already obtained ones. In line with this, we propose a conjecture about the coefficients in a basis similar to {\\stackrel{̄}{a}}_{k}\\left(I\\right).\n\nConjecture 6.4\n\nFor the peak-polynomial, the coefficients in basis {\\left\\{\\left(\\genfrac{}{}{0ex}{}{n-m+k}{k+1}\\right)\\right\\}}_{k\\in \\mathbb{N}} form a symmetric, log-concave sequence of nonnegative integers.\n\n## CRediT authorship contribution statement\n\nFerenc Bencs: Conceptualized, Made the proofs, Wrote the manuscript.\n\n## Acknowledgments\n\nI would like to express my sincere gratitude to Bruce Sagan, who pointed out some corollaries of the behavior of different coefficient sequences. I would also like to thank Alexander Diaz-Lopez for his helpful remarks. The research was partially supported by the MTA Rényi Institute Lendület Limits of Structures Research Group, Hungary. The research leading to these results has received funding from the European Research Council under the European Union’s Seventh Framework Programme (FP7/2007-2013) / ERC grant agreement no. 617747.\n\n## References\n\n  1. [1]\n\nBilley S., Burdzy K., Sagan B.E.\n\nPermutations with given peak set\n\nJ. Integer Seq., 16 (6) (2013)\n\nGoogle Scholar\n\n  2. [2]\n\nBilley S., Fahrbach M., Talmage A.\n\nCoefficients and roots of peak polynomials\n\nExperiment. Math., 25 (2) (2016), pp. 165-175\n\nCrossrefView in ScopusGoogle Scholar\n\n  3. [3]\n\nBrenti F.\n\nUnimodal Log-Concave and Pólya Frequency Sequences in Combinatorics\n\nMemoirs of the AMS Series, American Mathematical Society (1989)\n\nGoogle Scholar\n\n  4. [4]\n\nDiaz-Lopez A., Harris P.E., Insko E., Omar M.\n\nA proof of the peak polynomial positivity conjecture\n\nJ. Combin. Theory Ser. A, 149 (2017), pp. 21-29\n\nView PDFView articleView in ScopusGoogle Scholar\n\n  5. [5]\n\nDiaz-Lopez A., Harris P.E., Insko E., Omar M., Sagan B.E.\n\nDescent polynomials\n\nDiscrete Math., 342 (6) (2019), pp. 1674-1686\n\nView PDFView articleView in ScopusGoogle Scholar\n\n  6. [6]\n\nMacMahon P.A.\n\nCombinatory Analysis\n\nDover Books on Mathematics Series, Dover Publications (2004)\n\nGoogle Scholar\n\n  7. [7]\n\nNeumaier A.\n\nEnclosing clusters of zeros of polynomials\n\nJ. Comput. Appl. Math., 156 (2) (2003), pp. 389-401\n\nView PDFView articleView in ScopusGoogle Scholar\n\n  8. [8]\n\nSolomon L.\n\nA Mackey formula in the group ring of a coxeter group\n\nJ. Algebra, 41 (2) (1976), pp. 255-264\n\nView PDFView articleView in ScopusGoogle Scholar\n\n  9. [9]\n\nStanley R.P.\n\nTwo combinatorial applications of the Aleksandrov-Fenchel inequalities\n\nJ. Combin. Theory Ser. A, 31 (1) (1981), pp. 56-65\n\nView PDFView articleView in ScopusGoogle Scholar\n\n  10. [10]\n\nStanley R.P.\n\nTwo poset polytopes\n\nDiscrete & Computational Geometry, 1 (1) (1986), pp. 9-23\n\nView in ScopusGoogle Scholar\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCumulative Subtraction Games (https://www.combinatorics.org/ojs/index.php/eljc/article/view/v26i4p52)\nciteturn0search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.9 years ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; We study a variation of Nim-type subtraction games, called Cumulative Subtraction (CS). ... We prove that the zero-sum outcome in optimal play of a CS with a finite number of possible actions is eventually periodic, with period $2s$, where $s$ is the size of the largest available action. This settles a conjecture by Stewart in his Ph.D. thesis (2011).\n\n# Cumulative Subtraction Games\n\n  * Gal Cohensius\n  * Urban Larsson\n  * Reshef Meir\n  * David Wahlstedt\n\nDOI: https://doi.org/10.37236/7904\n\n### Abstract\n\nWe study a variation of Nim-type subtraction games, called Cumulative Subtraction (CS). Two players alternate in removing pebbles out of a joint pile, and their actions add or remove points to a common score. We prove that the zero-sum outcome in optimal play of a CS with a finite number of possible actions is eventually periodic, with period $2s$, where $s$ is the size of the largest available action. This settles a conjecture by Stewart in his Ph.D. thesis (2011). Specifically, we find a quadratic bound, in the size of $s$, on when the outcome function must have become periodic. In case of exactly two possible actions, we give an explicit description of optimal play.\n\nPublished\n\n2019-12-20\n\nHow to Cite\n\nCohensius, G., Larsson, U., Meir, R., & Wahlstedt, D. (2019). Cumulative Subtraction Games. The Electronic Journal of Combinatorics, 26(4), #P4.52. https://doi.org/10.37236/7904\n\nIssue\n\nVolume 26, Issue 4 (2019)\n\nArticle Number\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCumulative subtraction games (https://arxiv.org/abs/1805.09368)\nciteturn0academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8.5 years ago; We study zero-sum games, a variant of the classical combinatorial Subtraction games (studied for example in the monumental work \"Winning Ways\", by Berlekamp, Conway and Guy), called Cumulative Subtraction (CS). ... We prove that the outcome in optimal play (game value) of a CS with a finite number of possible actions is eventually periodic, with period $2s$, where $s$ is the size of the largest available action. This settles a conjecture by Stewart in his Ph.D. thesis (2011). ... We generalize the periodicity result to games with a so-called reward function, where at each stage of game, the change of `score' does not necessarily equal the number of pebbles you collect.\nTitle: Cumulative subtraction games\nAuthors: Gal Cohensius, Urban Larsson, Reshef Meir, David Wahlstedt\nDate: Wed May 23 18:22:49 2018\n\nWe study zero-sum games, a variant of the classical combinatorial Subtraction games (studied for example in the monumental work \"Winning Ways\", by Berlekamp, Conway and Guy), called Cumulative Subtraction (CS). Two players alternate in moving, and get points for taking pebbles out of a joint pile. We prove that the outcome in optimal play (game value) of a CS with a finite number of possible actions is eventually periodic, with period $2s$, where $s$ is the size of the largest available action. This settles a conjecture by Stewart in his Ph.D. thesis (2011). Specifically, we find a quadratic bound, in the size of $s$, on when the outcome function must have become periodic. In case of two possible actions, we give an explicit description of optimal play. We generalize the periodicity result to games with a so-called reward function, where at each stage of game, the change of `score' does not necessarily equal the number of pebbles you collect.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCumulative subtraction games (https://www.combinatorics.org/ojs/index.php/eljc/article/download/v26i4p52/pdf/)\nciteturn0search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.9 years ago; We study a variation of Nim-type subtraction games, called Cumulative Sub- ... ally periodic, with period 2s, where s is the size of the largest available action. ... settles a conjecture by Stewart in his Ph.D. thesis (2011).\nCumulative subtraction games\nGal Cohensius\nUrban Larsson\nReshef Meir\nThe Faculty of Industrial Engineering and Management\nTechnion–Israel Institute of Technology\nHaifa, Israel\n{galcohensius@, reshefm@ie}.technion.ac.il\nurban031@gmail.com\nDavid Wahlstedt\nG¨oteborg, Sweden\ndavid.wahlstedt@gmail.com\nSubmitted: May 31, 2018; Accepted: Nov 29, 2019; Published: Dec 20, 2019\nc⃝The authors. Released under the CC BY-ND license (International 4.0).\nAbstract\nWe study a variation of Nim-type subtraction games, called Cumulative Sub-\ntraction (CS). Two players alternate in removing pebbles out of a joint pile, and\ntheir actions add or remove points to a common score. We prove that the zero-sum\noutcome in optimal play of a CS with a ﬁnite number of possible actions is eventu-\nally periodic, with period 2s, where s is the size of the largest available action. This\nsettles a conjecture by Stewart in his Ph.D. thesis (2011). Speciﬁcally, we ﬁnd a\nquadratic bound, in the size of s, on when the outcome function must have become\nperiodic. In case of exactly two possible actions, we give an explicit description of\noptimal play.\nMathematics Subject Classiﬁcations: 05C88, 05C89\n1\nIntroduction\nTwo players, Alice and Bob, stand next to a single pile of 7 pebbles, alternately taking\npebbles from it. They compete on who takes most pebbles. However there is a restriction\non the number of pebbles they may take each turn; on e